EldenNet, the read-only round (package @LABEL@)
@UNDERLINE@

This package runs a read-only round of EldenNet's address library (Lodestar; EldenNet's decisions O100 to O102) on
your own PC. The two offline runs you made read the game's executable file and found the 217 places where the game
keeps its main objects (EldenNet calls them managers: one keeps the world's characters, another the time of day, and
so on), without knowing which is which. This round starts the game with EldenNet loaded and reads, from
the game as it runs, which of those places holds which of the eleven managers EldenNet needs. Then it tries, for a few
minutes, the way EldenNet v1 will read the game: from a thread of its own, about ten times a second.

It only reads. It calls nothing in the game, adds nothing to it and changes nothing in it. There is no key to press.
Nothing in this package is for playing with yet.

Built from EldenNet's engine branch at commit @COMMIT@ with clang-cl (Release). Never shared: a test build for you
alone, labelled @LABEL@.


How long it takes
-----------------

About 15 minutes: 5 to unzip and start, 1 to 2 at the title screen, about 5 in the world with your save loaded, and a
few to send the files.


What is in it
-------------

  read.cmd                Double-click this to start the round.
  eldennet-launcher.exe   EldenNet's launcher: backs up your saves, starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat,
                          loads EldenNet, and watches the game until it ends.
  eldennet.dll            EldenNet in the game: the probe build, which runs this round and nothing else.
  eldennet-engine.exe     EldenNet's command-line tool, here to check the report (its lint command).
  gal\                    What the round works from: the build's fingerprint (builds.toml), how a manager's place is
                          recognised (recipes.toml, with its note in derivations\), EldenNet's own numbers and labels
                          for the places it needs (ids.toml), and what to look for (leads.toml). Numbers and names of
                          the design's own, nothing taken from the game.
  README-READ.txt         This file.
  SHA256SUMS              The SHA-256 of every other file in this package.
  LICENSE.txt             EldenNet's licence (MIT).
  THIRD_PARTY.md          What EldenNet is built with, and its credits.
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt The licences of the libraries inside the programs.


Before you start
----------------

  - Close the game, and start Steam.
  - Unzip this package into a folder of its own, anywhere but the game's folder.
  - The launcher backs up your saves before it starts the game (in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups), and the round
    does not run without that backup. If you want a copy of your own as well, copy the folder %APPDATA%\EldenRing
    somewhere first.
  - The game will run offline, without Easy Anti-Cheat. Use a save you are happy to play offline, and never take a save
    played with mods into online play.
  - The launcher's messages appear in a console window behind the game. Setting the game to windowed mode first makes
    them easy to read; otherwise press Alt+Tab to look, and Alt+Tab back.
  - Windows or your security software may warn about an unsigned program. Allow it only if the SHA-256 values match
    (below); otherwise stop and tell us.


The steps
---------

1. Double-click read.cmd. (Or, in a command prompt in this folder: eldennet-launcher.exe --read)

   The first time, the launcher asks where eldenring.exe is if it cannot find it through Steam, and shows its notices.

2. The game starts. At the title screen, wait. The console says, in lines starting with "EldenNet read:", that it is
   reading the game. This takes about a minute. Do not load a save until it tells you to.

3. The console then says "Now load your save." with how many of the eleven managers it has named so far (some exist
   only once a save is loaded), or, if all eleven are there already, "All 11 managers are named. If you have not
   loaded your save yet, load it now." Either way, load your save. Walk about for about five minutes: go near an NPC
   or an enemy, open the map, rest at a grace, and fast travel once. Every ten seconds the round looks again for the
   managers it has not named yet, and all the while it reads the ones it has.

4. After about five minutes in the world, quit the game as you usually do, whatever the console has said. The console
   then says whether the two files passed their check, and the launcher ends.

5. Send these files back to us:

     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-found.json   required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-table.toml   required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\eldennet.log           helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\launcher.log           helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\crashes\                    the newest file there, only if the game crashed

   (Type %APPDATA%\EldenNet into Explorer's address bar to open the folder.) You can check the report yourself:

     eldennet-engine.exe lint --report "%APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-found.json"

   It must say "lint: the report is clean". The launcher has checked it the same way already.

If anything else happens (the console says the build is not supported, or that the round stopped, or the game closes),
do not try again: send the two logs and tell us what the console said. That is a useful result too.


What it does
------------

  - Backs up your saves (it only copies them), checks that Steam is running and that the game is not, and checks that
    your game is the build the offline runs read (2.7.2.0, worldwide). If the launcher says the build is not
    supported, stop: the game has been updated since, and the offline run must be made again first.
  - Starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat, by writing the game's Steam app id into steam_appid.txt beside
    the game while it starts, and removing it again (eldennet-launcher.exe --cleanup removes it if a crash left it).
  - Loads eldennet.dll into the game. In the game, EldenNet reads the game's code and data as they are loaded,
    read-only and through guarded copies, and finds again the 217 places the offline runs found.
  - For each of those places it reads the address kept there and, where there is one, the first eight bytes of the
    object at that address, once. Those eight bytes say what kind of object it is, and the game's own type
    information, already read from its code, gives that kind a class. Where the class is one of the eleven EldenNet
    asks for, the place is that manager's. It does this again every ten seconds, for at most ten minutes, while any of
    the eleven is still missing.
  - From a thread of its own, about ten times a second, it reads each named manager's place and the first eight bytes
    of its object again, and counts: how often the place was empty, how often the object was still of the same kind,
    how often a read was refused, and how long a reading took. This is the way EldenNet v1 will read the game, tried
    here on sixteen bytes a manager.
  - Every two seconds it writes lodestar-found.json into %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe, and lodestar-table.toml when
    another manager is named. They hold only numbers (offsets, sizes, counts) and EldenNet's own labels: no names of
    the game's, no text, no paths, nothing of your character or your PC.
  - Watches the game until it ends, and keeps a short record if it ends badly: the error's code, which module it was
    in and the time, never a memory dump.


What it never does
------------------

  - It never calls anything in the game, never adds anything to the game's own lists, and never changes the game's
    code or data. It reads them.
  - It never goes online, and the game cannot reach FromSoftware's servers without Easy Anti-Cheat.
  - It never changes your saves or the game's files (steam_appid.txt aside, which it removes).
  - It never runs anything else, uses the network, keeps keys or sends anything anywhere. You send the files yourself.


If the game crashes or freezes
------------------------------

  - Your saves were backed up before the game started: each backup is a folder in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups,
    named by date and time, with a manifest of the files' sizes and hashes. The game's own save is not touched by
    EldenNet; only if the game itself reports a damaged save, copy ER0000.sl2 (and ER0000.sl2.bak) from the newest
    backup back into %APPDATA%\EldenRing\<the same number>\, with the game closed.
  - If the game freezes, close it with Alt+F4, or end eldenring.exe in Task Manager.
  - Send the files of step 5, the newest crash record included. The next start of the launcher explains it once.
  - To play normally again, start the game from Steam as usual. Nothing of EldenNet stays loaded.


Check the files
---------------

In PowerShell, in this folder:

  Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 eldennet-launcher.exe, eldennet.dll, eldennet-engine.exe

Each value must equal its line in SHA256SUMS (letter case aside).


